---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, url, repoUrl } from '../config';
---

<Base title="About" description="What Ahead of Main covers, how it's researched, and how to contribute.">
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>About Ahead of Main</h1>
    <p>{SITE.tagline}</p>
  </header>

  <div class="issue-body">
    <p>
      Most developer newsletters tell you what shipped. Ahead of Main looks at the layer underneath: early signals that
      tend to reach the headlines weeks later, capabilities you can adopt this week, and shifts in what software
      work is worth. If a story was on the front page of Hacker News, it's only here if there's an angle the headline
      missed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The name comes from git: a branch that's ahead of main has commits main hasn't merged yet. The first four
      issues were published as DEV Ahead.
    </p>

    <h2 id="whats-in-an-issue">What's in an issue</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The top 3.</strong> What changed, and what it means for how you work.</li>
      <li><strong>Signal radar.</strong> Early signals, each with a confidence level.</li>
      <li><strong>Research frontier.</strong> Papers explained in plain language, with an honest time horizon.</li>
      <li><strong>New capabilities.</strong> Things you can wire up this week, with a rough time cost.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Disruption watch.</strong> A running board, rated on a four-step ladder. See the <a
          href={url('/watch')}>current board</a
        >.
      </li>
      <li><strong>This week's experiment.</strong> A 30–90 minute build with a clear finish line.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Items are tagged <span class="tag tag-act">ACT NOW</span> (try it this week), <span class="tag tag-watch"
        >WATCH</span
      > (likely to matter, not yet actionable) or <span class="tag tag-context">CONTEXT</span> (worth knowing).
    </p>

    <h2 id="how-its-made">How it's made</h2>
    <p>
      Research for each issue is done with an AI agent that reads changelogs, release notes, papers and community
      digests, and keeps the Disruption Watch board consistent from issue to issue. I edit, check and add to every
      issue before it's published. Where a claim comes from a secondary source or couldn't be verified, the issue
      says so.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contribute">Contribute</h2>
    <p>
      {
        repoUrl ? (
          <>
            Every issue lives in a <a href={repoUrl}>public GitHub repository</a>. If you think something is wrong, a
            rating should move, or there's a signal worth covering, <a href={`${repoUrl}/issues/new`}>open an issue</a>.
          </>
        ) : (
          <>Every issue lives in a public GitHub repository, where you can suggest items or challenge a rating.</>
        )
      }
    </p>

    <h2 id="follow">Follow</h2>
    <p>
      {
        SITE.linkedinNewsletter && (
          <>
            Subscribe to the <a href={SITE.linkedinNewsletter}>LinkedIn newsletter</a>, or{' '}
          </>
        )
      }
      use the <a href={url('/rss.xml')}>RSS feed</a> in any reader.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
